WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:05.760
<v Rayyan>Last time, AI was a promise made by scientists in the 1950s, in rooms full of machines most people never saw.

00:00:06.110 --> 00:00:15.950
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Today we jump to 1984, when computers moved into homes, schools and offices, and the question changed from "what will these machines become?" to "can normal people use them?"

00:00:16.300 --> 00:00:17.260
<v Rayyan>I'm Rayyan.

00:00:17.610 --> 00:00:18.877
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And I'm Abdulrahman.

00:00:18.877 --> 00:00:22.170
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>This is The Evolution of AI Perception, episode two.

00:00:23.170 --> 00:00:24.303
<v Rayyan>So set the scene.

00:00:24.303 --> 00:00:26.770
<v Rayyan>What were computers like before 1984?

00:00:27.120 --> 00:00:28.889
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Complex and difficult to use.

00:00:28.889 --> 00:00:31.755
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>That's how the Steve Jobs Archive describes it.

00:00:31.755 --> 00:00:34.317
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>The Macintosh team set out to change that.

00:00:34.317 --> 00:00:39.440
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Their goal was the first mass-market personal computer that was truly user-friendly.

00:00:39.790 --> 00:00:41.710
<v Rayyan>And Steve Jobs was running that team?

00:00:42.060 --> 00:00:43.560
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He'd managed it since 1981.

00:00:43.560 --> 00:00:48.060
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And he had a phrase for what they were building: "a computer for the rest of us."

00:00:48.410 --> 00:00:50.090
<v Rayyan>Meaning not just for engineers.

00:00:50.440 --> 00:00:50.792
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Right.

00:00:50.792 --> 00:00:52.669
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And the team itself was unusual.

00:00:52.669 --> 00:00:57.947
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Engineers, designers and programmers who were also sculptors, photographers and musicians.

00:00:57.947 --> 00:01:03.871
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Susan Kare designed the fonts and icons, and her smiling Mac logo made the machine feel almost human.

00:01:03.871 --> 00:01:07.800
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Bill Atkinson's graphics software was key to making it easy to use.

00:01:08.150 --> 00:01:10.070
<v Rayyan>And they built on other people's ideas too.

00:01:10.420 --> 00:01:17.460
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Yes: Jef Raskin, who'd been an Apple manager, and researchers at Xerox PARC and the Stanford Research Institute.

00:01:17.810 --> 00:01:19.250
<v Rayyan>Did Jobs think it would work?

00:01:19.600 --> 00:01:25.840
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He told the team that within five years, somebody would be "using a Macintosh for something we never dreamed possible."

00:01:26.190 --> 00:01:27.550
<v Rayyan>And did the team believe him?

00:01:27.900 --> 00:01:30.977
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In 2007, Jobs remembered the week before the launch.

00:01:30.977 --> 00:01:35.889
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He said the team got together and agreed: every computer is going to work this way.

00:01:35.889 --> 00:01:40.860
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>You could argue about how long it would take, but not about whether it would happen.

00:01:41.210 --> 00:01:44.650
<v Rayyan>Pictures, icons, a pointer instead of typed commands.

00:01:45.000 --> 00:01:45.222
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Yes.

00:01:45.222 --> 00:01:48.944
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And think about what that does to how people feel about a computer.

00:01:48.944 --> 00:01:51.056
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Before, you had to learn its language.

00:01:51.056 --> 00:01:53.000
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Now it was designed to learn yours.

00:01:54.000 --> 00:01:57.318
<v Rayyan>Jobs had a famous way of explaining what computers were for.

00:01:57.318 --> 00:02:01.520
<v Rayyan>In a 1990 film about the Library of Congress, he told a story about a study.

00:02:01.870 --> 00:02:05.202
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Scientists measured how efficiently different animals move.

00:02:05.202 --> 00:02:08.308
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>The condor used the least energy to travel a kilometre.

00:02:08.308 --> 00:02:08.703
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Humans?

00:02:08.703 --> 00:02:10.905
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>About a third of the way down the list.

00:02:10.905 --> 00:02:11.470
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Not great.

00:02:11.820 --> 00:02:13.900
<v Rayyan>But then someone tested a human on a bicycle.

00:02:14.250 --> 00:02:16.862
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And the human on a bicycle blew the condor away.

00:02:16.862 --> 00:02:24.970
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Jobs said that's what a computer is to him: "the most remarkable tool that we've ever come up with," and "the equivalent of a bicycle for our minds."

00:02:25.320 --> 00:02:29.240
<v Rayyan>I like that, because a bicycle is powerful, but it only goes where you steer it.

00:02:29.590 --> 00:02:31.370
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>That's the image of this whole era.

00:02:31.370 --> 00:02:35.590
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Computers were presented as tools that make you stronger while you stay in control.

00:02:36.590 --> 00:02:38.836
<v Rayyan>So computers were getting friendlier.

00:02:38.836 --> 00:02:40.110
<v Rayyan>What about AI itself?

00:02:40.460 --> 00:02:41.148
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Struggling.

00:02:41.148 --> 00:02:49.660
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Around 1980 there was a boom in "expert systems," programs built for one specific area, like identifying objects or diagnosing diseases.

00:02:50.010 --> 00:02:50.810
<v Rayyan>And then?

00:02:51.160 --> 00:02:52.892
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In 1987, the second AI winter.

00:02:52.892 --> 00:02:57.800
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Expert systems kept failing whenever they met information they hadn't been built for.

00:02:58.150 --> 00:03:02.950
<v Rayyan>So while the public was falling in love with personal computers, AI was going through a quiet patch.

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:06.065
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And it came back in narrower forms.

00:03:06.065 --> 00:03:11.122
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In the 1990s, researchers moved to data-driven machine learning.

00:03:11.122 --> 00:03:16.020
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And in May 1997, IBM's Deep Blue beat Garry Kasparov at chess.

00:03:16.370 --> 00:03:17.890
<v Rayyan>The prediction from last episode.

00:03:18.240 --> 00:03:19.755
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Almost forty years late.

00:03:19.755 --> 00:03:20.954
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>And it wasn't easy.

00:03:20.954 --> 00:03:26.320
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In their first match, Deep Blue won one game, but Kasparov won the match four to two.

00:03:26.670 --> 00:03:29.070
<v Rayyan>So the 1997 one was a rematch.

00:03:29.420 --> 00:03:36.485
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>In New York, and IBM says it was widely seen as a symbolic test of whether supercomputers were catching up with human intelligence.

00:03:36.485 --> 00:03:41.500
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Kasparov won the first game, Deep Blue the second, and Deep Blue won the deciding sixth game.

00:03:41.850 --> 00:03:43.530
<v Rayyan>How did Kasparov react?

00:03:43.880 --> 00:03:48.592
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>He said that for the first time he "saw something similar to an artificial intellect."

00:03:48.592 --> 00:03:51.880
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>After that, Deep Blue was retired to the Smithsonian museum.

00:03:52.230 --> 00:03:57.030
<v Rayyan>So most people met AI in this era through news events like that, not through anything they used themselves.

00:03:58.030 --> 00:03:58.681
<v Rayyan>Then 2011.

00:03:58.681 --> 00:04:02.910
<v Rayyan>On October 4th, Apple announced the iPhone 4S, and with it, Siri.

00:04:03.260 --> 00:04:08.700
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Apple described Siri as "an intelligent assistant that helps you get things done just by asking."

00:04:09.050 --> 00:04:11.210
<v Rayyan>And the big deal was that you could talk to it normally.

00:04:11.560 --> 00:04:11.903
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Right.

00:04:11.903 --> 00:04:18.239
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Apple's example: ask "Will I need an umbrella this weekend?" and it understood you wanted the weather forecast.

00:04:18.239 --> 00:04:22.920
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Or say "Remind me to call Mom when I get home," and it found Mom in your contacts.

00:04:23.270 --> 00:04:28.070
<v Rayyan>It could send messages and emails, set reminders, make calls, search the internet and give directions.

00:04:28.420 --> 00:04:32.904
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Apple also said Siri was smart about the personal information you allow it to access.

00:04:32.904 --> 00:04:38.020
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Ask "What's the traffic like around here?" and it worked out where "here" was from your location.

00:04:38.370 --> 00:04:40.210
<v Rayyan>Which is a lot of trust to give a phone.

00:04:40.560 --> 00:04:47.840
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>It launched in beta, in English, French and German, and the iPhone 4S went on sale on October 14th in seven countries.

00:04:48.190 --> 00:04:51.630
<v Rayyan>So for the first time, a lot of normal people had AI in their pocket.

00:04:52.630 --> 00:04:54.445
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>So compare this with the 1950s.

00:04:54.445 --> 00:04:56.963
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Back then, AI was a promise you read about.

00:04:56.963 --> 00:04:58.310
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Now it's in your phone.

00:04:58.660 --> 00:04:59.987
<v Rayyan>But notice how it arrived.

00:04:59.987 --> 00:05:02.284
<v Rayyan>Inside a friendly interface, as an assistant.

00:05:02.284 --> 00:05:03.712
<v Rayyan>It does things when you ask.

00:05:03.712 --> 00:05:04.580
<v Rayyan>It waits for you.

00:05:04.930 --> 00:05:06.530
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Just like Jobs's bicycle.

00:05:06.530 --> 00:05:07.810
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>You do the steering.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:11.388
<v Rayyan>And that set an expectation that AI is something we direct.

00:05:11.388 --> 00:05:15.600
<v Rayyan>Next episode, we look at what happens when AI stops waiting for instructions.

00:05:15.950 --> 00:05:19.048
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Thanks for listening to The Evolution of AI Perception.

00:05:19.048 --> 00:05:21.470
<v Abdulrahman Ahmad>Explore the Jobs Era island on our website.
